# Law Students Civil Rights Research Council

**Authors:** 

## Abstract
<p>Chapter 1 focuses on the Law Students Civil Right Research Council to examine the role of LSCRRC in expanding the ranks of civil rights lawyers. From 1963 to 1987, LSCRRC placed nearly 5000 law students in civil rights internships and clerkships throughout the South. Marian Wright Edelman was one of the co-founders, and notable individuals such as Hillary Clinton and Justice Clarence Thomas have cited experiences with the organization as influencing their careers. Vernon Crawford, one of five Black lawyers in Alabama in the mid-1960s, and C B King, a Black attorney in Albany, GA, feature in the chapter as towering mentors whose front-line work in civil rights litigation inspired many law student interns.</p>

**Source:** https://doi.org/10.5744/florida/9780813079165.003.0002

